#{[17592186069996 "Given a quadratic equation in the form \n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0,\n$$\n\nwe can find its roots using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{\\color{blue}b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this formula, the expression under the square root is called the **discriminant**:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = {\\color{blue}b^2 - 4ac}\n$$\n\nThe value of the discriminant $\\mathcal{D}$ tells us a lot about the solutions of our quadratic equation.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}>0,$ then we get $2$ distinct real solutions: one with the positive square root and one with the negative square root.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}=0,$ then we get $1$ real solution because the square root vanishes. We sometimes say that the equation has two equal solutions.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}<0,$ then we get no real solutions.\n\nLet's illustrate with some examples:\n\n- If $x^2+3x+2=0,$ then we have a positive discriminant $\\mathcal{D}=3^2-4(1)(2) = 1$ and $2$ distinct real solutions:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{3^2-4(1)(2)} }{2(1)} = \\dfrac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{1} }{2} = \\dfrac{-3 \\pm 1}{2} = -2,-1\n$$\n\n- If $x^2+2x+1=0,$ then we have a zero discriminant $\\mathcal{D}=2^2-4(1)(1) = 0$ and $1$ distinct real solution:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{2^2-4(1)(1)} }{2(1)} = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{0} }{2} = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm 0}{2} = -1\n$$\n\n- If $x^2+x+1=0,$ then we have a negative discriminant $\\mathcal{D}=1^2-4(1)(1) = -3$ and no real solutions:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1^2-4(1)(1)} }{2(1)} = \\dfrac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3} }{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069997 "Suppose we have the quadratic equation \n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the coefficients $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are all *rational numbers,* and\n\n- the discriminant is non-negative, i.e., $\\mathcal D \\geq 0.$\n\nThen, we have the following possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}$ is a *perfect square*, then the equation has two *rational* solutions.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}$ is *not* a perfect square, then the equation has two *irrational* solutions.\n\nLet's illustrate with some examples.\n\nConsider the equation $x^2-x-6=0.$ Its discriminant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-1)^{2}-4(1)(-6) \\\\  & =1+24 \\\\  & =25 \\\\  & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a perfect square. Consequently, the corresponding solutions are both rational numbers:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{5^2}}{2(1)} = 3, -2\n$$\n\nNow, consider the equation $x^2-x-5=0.$ Its discriminant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-1)^{2}-4(1)(-5) \\\\  & =1+20 \\\\  & =21,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a perfect square. Consequently, the solutions are both irrational numbers:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{21}}{2}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069998 "To simplify radical expressions, we can use the quotient rule for radicals, which states \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\sqrt {a}} {\\sqrt{b}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac a b}.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's simplify $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16w^2}{25}}$\n\nFirst, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16w^2}{25}}  = \\dfrac {\\sqrt{16w^2}} {\\color{blue}\\sqrt{25}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{16w^2}}{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5^2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{16w^2}}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression under the radical as a perfect square and taking its square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{16{w}^{2}}}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(4w)^{2}}}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{|4w|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{|4|\\cdot |w|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{4\\cdot |w|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{4|w|}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069999 "The quotient rule for radicals also works for higher-order roots, like the cube root:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{ \\dfrac a b } = \\dfrac{ \\sqrt[3]{a} }{ \\sqrt[3]{b} }\n$$\n\nIn general, we have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{ \\dfrac a b } = \\dfrac{ \\sqrt[n]{a} }{ \\sqrt[n]{b} }\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070000 "Sometimes, we must solve an absolute value equation containing variables on both sides. For example,\n\n$$\n\\left\\vert 5x +7\\right\\vert = 3x +9\n$$\n\nGeometrically, these solutions correspond to the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points of the curves\n\n$$\ny= \\mid 5x+7 \\mid\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=3x+9\n$$\n\nshown as the points $P$ and $Q$ below.\n\n![](images/8f/8f2e2934c07824288ec9001f4fc42d7e5356597402bddcf4df3c79ade5351e22.png)\n\n**Note:** *Image not drawn to scale.*\n\nLet's now discuss how to find these intersection points algebraically." #edb/ref 13194139533349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070001 "We wish to find the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\vert 5x + 7 \\vert = 3x +9\n$$\n\nFor this equation to be true, either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nTherefore, we have the following two cases:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}5x+7 & = 5x+7 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad & 5x+7\\end{vmatrix} = -(5x+7)\n$$\n\nWe now consider these two cases separately:\n\n- If $\\mid 5x +7|=5x +7$, we obtain the following equation:\n$5x +7 = 3x +9$\nWe now solve this equation using the usual methods:\n$5x + 7|= 3x + 9; 2x + 7|= 9; 2x|= 2; x|= 1$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$\\mid 5x + 7 \\mid |= 3x + 9; \\mid 5(1) + 7 \\mid |= 3(1) + 9; \\mid 5 + 7 \\mid |= 3 + 9; \\mid 12 \\mid |= 12; 12|= 12✓$\nSince $x=1$ satisfies the original equation, it's a valid solution.\n- If $\\mid 5x +7|=-(5x +7)$, we obtain the following equation:\n$-(5x +7)=3x +9$\nWe now solve this equation using the usual methods:\n$- (5x + 7)|= 3x + 9; - 5x - 7|= 3x + 9; - 7|= 8x + 9; - 16|= 8x; - 2|= x; x|=-2$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$\\mid 5x + 7 \\mid |= 3(-2) + 9; \\mid 5(-2) + 7 \\mid |= 3(-2) + 9; \\mid - 10 + 7 \\mid |=-6 + 9; \\mid - 3 \\mid |= 3; 3|= 3✓$\nSince $x=-2$ satisfies the original equation, it's a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solutions of our equation are $x=-2, \\, 1$.\n\n![](images/a8/a8982c526d460e2968b5e06871f74adc32da549faeffa89ac58a39c4d84730d5.png)" #edb/ref 13194139533349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070002 "A line that crosses two parallel lines is called a **transversal**. For example, the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$ in the diagram below is a transversal.\n\n![](images/cd/cdae3e21ca7b66c756e047b2f71dd03d1320a947e7477f6b025dacef95321399.png)\n\nA pair of parallel lines with a transversal form a set of $8$ angles, shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4465bbe051f978925e704cc40ced894c4e8c6625c53c7bd944a93c123359ab9.png)\n\nThis set of eight angles can be grouped into two categories:\n\n- We have four pairs of **vertical angles**:\n\n![](images/e0/e010db0c586e29e8ae640842f1519da314455bb7520680b80530799509058df3.png)\n\n- We also have four pairs of so-called **corresponding angles**:\n\n![](images/d1/d1b3f1ede08e3c20c80b7a23477491237e73a9b6691d93e6deabc5e5a4e0c57b.png)\n\nCorresponding pairs of angles are located in the same position as each other relative to the parallel lines and on the same side of the transversal. Pairs of corresponding angles are congruent." #edb/ref 13194139535339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070003 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^{x - y}\\,.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of both $x$ and $y,$ so we can't solve the equation by integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ at least not straight away. So, how do we find a general solution to the equation?\n\nFirst of all, note that it is possible to separate the variables $x$ and $y$ on each side of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x-y}=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{y}}\\,⟹\\,{e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the rule for integration by substitution, we can simplify the integrand on the left-hand side and carry out the integration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & ={e}^{x}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ne^y  \t= e^x + C.\n$$\n\nThis is an **implicit form** of the solution since it is not written explicitly as $y=y(x).$\n\nWe can write our general solution in **explicit form** (i.e., in the form $y=y(x)$) by noting that since $e^y > 0$ for all $y$, we may assume that the right-hand side is positive and apply the natural logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}({e}^{x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}({e}^{x}+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070004 "The differential equation that we just solved was **separable**. A first-order differential equation is separable if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{g(y)} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x).\n$$\n\nOnce the variables are separated, we can integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{g(y)} & =\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then evaluate both integrals and find the solution. Note that for the solution to be valid, we require $g(y)\\neq 0.$" #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070005 "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the graph of $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/99cee7824a157e5a08292ebba9e60a5633c7a2f8a5ebb496fc1d5dbe14f858e3.png)\n\nWe can *approximate* the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1.$\n\n![](images/8a/8aa7a3f9be8371b1459874cd2cb8c0c4caf1000d76b99d134fb6e806e638c548.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\overset{\\text{[math]1[/math]st rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]2[/math]nd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]3[/math]rd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(3)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{2}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{3}^{2}\\cdot 1] \\\\  & =1+4+9 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **left Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*left* corner of each rectangle touches the curve. \n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4],$ the rectangles are entirely *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area." #edb/ref 13194139535868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070006 "If ![](images/9a/9ac10abedf6cecc066a4858ab2859e3c3038a43b10ac3261bd4120d0dc861990.svg) on ![](images/88/88e5bb9f44a0b3bb16a7e0e5461c5cc3f1f0bef907eda396d725caf367f29fb5.svg) the area ![](images/6b/6bd69882ede929379fa9babdf49386dd09138f26cabc6a4d617bbac5e92d21a5.svg) under the curve ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) over the interval ![](images/2f/2f2185d0962aceaafb8dbacf7486e61ccafbb77a54e961b33e886dd3b79a20df.svg) can be approximated using a left Riemann sum by calculating the area of each rectangle and adding them together.\n\n![](images/85/8547bd96f866100db3ed28f3f23e3446ab5eff50632c425c23c2916c89f823db.png)\n\n  \n\nWe can write this mathematically as\n\n![](images/c9/c92cf3627eeb4798f2f72577c3df4788c311b59b575ccbfa17caf14f39dacff1.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/4a/4a4a2bd99240362f13e0954449f58ab1c21ae7d29ffec2a1b2c8f1350f6fbec7.svg) and ![](images/09/0979b76b241c9ddb5f2e3d097811c00175e0f9242fa36cc4bff45e2963c3dd8f.svg) are the endpoints of the interval, ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) is the number of rectangles, and the step size ![](images/bd/bd77198ba2d349a9ae25a0866060aa94624d6e255cabe04e818dc06e755ae85c.svg) is given by\n![](images/c9/c9f5c5a2741bae825236da3287d754a2526954e894011ace34f5cde5e65a4b45.svg)\n\nWe can simplify by factoring out the ![](images/a7/a7e281a725fb2bff766e9ddb72c85bb6f7a8e7d45de6212372795ba1764d5ddc.svg) which gives the following general formula:\n\n![](images/27/277114008d8847a523fb417695fe2f62a4f7ff08efc0b9b8ea58556029ea9c5e.svg)" #edb/ref 13194139535868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070007 "In the last few examples, the step size $\\Delta x$ was the same for each of the rectangles. In these cases, we say that the step size is **regular**.\n\nThere are situations where the step size can vary, which we call an **irregular step size**. But we can still use a left Riemann sum to approximate the area.\n\nFor example, let's use a left Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n\nFirst, note that the domain of $f(x)$ has been *partitioned* as \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n [1,4] &= [1, 2] \\cup [2, 3.5] \\cup [3.5, 4] .\n}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table. \n\n![](images/2e/2e8563480f46ff335042c3c52a6f60983f871dccb886c8cf3ca0844504a73db6.png)\n\n  \n\nWe've drawn some rectangles to help us calculate our left Riemann sum, and we have chosen the heights of each rectangle to be calculated using the left endpoint of each interval. \n\nBy calculating the area of each rectangle, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n![](images/8f/8f62020b1888f7479665b04028acb822fdccfa9049a37e306ea30ce0aa703ac7.svg)\n\nPlugging our numbers into the above, we find\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\mathcal{A}\t&\\approx\t{\\color{red}1} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}2-1})\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ {\\color{red}4} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}3.5-2})\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ {\\color{red}6} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}4-3.5}) \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= {\\color{red}1} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1} +{\\color{red}4} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1.5}+ {\\color{red}6} \\cdot {\\color{blue}0.5}  \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&=1+6+3\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= 10.\n}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070008 "Consider the following pair of parallel lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD},$ and the transversal $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}.$\n\n![](images/af/af403ef4827e840a49d5a3bde36561d22a508d262b3ad15b830812aefc306286.png)\n\n What is the value of $x?$\n\nFirst, note that $\\angle DIF$ and $\\angle BJI$ are corresponding angles. Therefore, they are congruent and thus have the same measure. This means we can write down the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DIF & =m∠BJI \\\\ 3x-{20}^{∘} & =x+{10}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we first subtract $x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{20}^{∘} & =x+{10}^{∘} \\\\ 3x-{20}^{∘}-x & =x+{10}^{∘}-x \\\\ 2x-{20}^{∘} & ={10}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $20^\\circ$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-{20}^{∘} & ={10}^{∘} \\\\ 2x-{20}^{∘}+{20}^{∘} & ={10}^{∘}+{20}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={30}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & =\\frac{{30}^{∘}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} & ={15}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=15^\\circ.$" #edb/ref 13194139535339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070009 "Suppose that we have the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx(t) = t+1, \\qquad y(t) = t^2+2, \\qquad t\\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThese parametric equations trace out a curve in the $xy$-plane. We can combine these into a single function, called a **vector-valued** function $\\mathbf r (t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r (t) = \\langle x(t), \\: y(t)\\rangle  =  \\langle t+1, \\: t^2+2\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe function $\\mathbf r (t)$ takes a **parameter** $t$ as input and returns a **position vector**. For a given value of $t,$ each value of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ has its tail at the origin and its terminal point at the coordinates \n$(t+1, \\: t^2+2).$\n\n![](images/10/1023d1a1898a402b95d8374f6242d494f714cde9c5ad4cb8186567b9ea192431.png)\n\nEvaluating vector-valued functions is straightforward. Just remember that the output is a vector, not a scalar. \n\nFor the vectors in the graph above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-3) & =⟨(-3)+1,\\,(-3)^{2}+2⟩=⟨-2,\\,11⟩, \\\\ r(0) & =⟨(0)+1,\\,(0)^{2}+2⟩=⟨1,\\,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨(2)+1,\\,(2)^{2}+2⟩=⟨3,\\,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070010 "The **magnitude** of a vector-valued function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left< x(t), \\: y(t) \\right>,\n$$\n\ndenoted $|\\mathbf{r}(t)|,$ is defined as follows:\n\n$$\n|\\mathbf{r}(t)| = \\sqrt{(x(t))^2+(y(t))^2}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude of a three-dimensional vector-valued function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left< x(t), \\: y(t), \\: z(t) \\right>\n$$\n\nis defined similarly:\n\n$$\n|\\mathbf{r}(t)| = \\sqrt{(x(t))^2+(y(t))^2+(z(t))^2}\n$$\n\n**Note:** An alternative notation that's often used for the magnitude of a vector-valued function is $||\\mathbf r(t) ||.$" #edb/ref 13194139533439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070011 "We can also define vector-valued functions with three components. For example,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} (t) = t \\ \\mathbf{i} + 2t^2 \\ \\mathbf{j} + 3t^3 \\ \\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nThis particular definition uses the so-called $\\mathbf{i}$-$\\mathbf{j}$-$\\mathbf{k}$ notation.\n\nWe can represent the same function using **angle bracket** notation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t) = \\langle t,  \\: 2t^2, \\: 3t^3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nAnother notation that's often convenient is **bracket** notation:\n\n![](images/a9/a95fd21b6ea5e7eae150b516d7d0ad82d283a31c895c9223abbeac81b622bb7d.svg)\n\nIt's important to realize that all three notations represent the same function." #edb/ref 13194139534609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070012 "The **$y$-intercept** of a line is where the line intersects the $y$-axis. For instance, consider the line that passes through the point $(1,-1)$ and has a slope of $3.$ How can we find the $y$-intercept of this line? \n\n![](images/c3/c307a60266b77cb497a5c5b02a6d9518a3b31e22b1ec97afb1d2b40f488982cb.png)\n\nWe already know values for $x$, $y$ and $m,$ so we can just substitute them into the equation $y=mx+b$ and then solve for $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =mx+b \\\\ (-1) & =(3)(1)+b \\\\ -1 & =3+b \\\\ b & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-intercept is ${\\color{blue}b}=-4.$ The coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $(0,-4).$" #edb/ref 13194139535291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070013 "The **$x$-intercept** of a line is where the line intersects the $x$-axis. For instance, consider the line $y = 2x - 4$. How can we find the $x$-intercept of this line?\n\n![](images/57/57a31eab932c948358e7ab6e428707a6a4b9f551bc956554c3a16b659b5f7988.png)\n\nAt the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0.$ So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-4 \\\\ (0) & =2x-4 \\\\ 4 & =2x \\\\ 2 & =x \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$**-intercept** is $x=2.$ The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(2,0).$" #edb/ref 13194139535291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070014 "How can we solve an integral like\n\n$$\n\\int 2x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x \\,?\n$$\n\nNotice that if we let $u=x^2+1$, then the derivative is $u' = 2x,$ and both $u$ and $u'$ are present in the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{2x}_{u'}(\\overbrace{x^2+1}^{u})^3\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIntegrals like this can always be solved using substitution. So, let's proceed. If we let ${\\color{red}{u=x^2+1}},$ then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\color{blue}\\textrm{d}u=2x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can now see that the change of variables from $x$ to $u$ is convenient because if we substitute, we get\n\n$$\n\\int {\\color{blue}{2x}}({\\color{red}{x^2+1}})^3\\,{\\color{blue}{\\textrm{d}x}} =  \\int \\color{red}(x^2+1)^{\\color{black}3}\\, \\color{blue}{2x\\,\\textrm{d}x} \\color{black}= \\int {\\color{red}u}^{\\color{black}3}\\, {\\color{blue}{\\,\\textrm{d}u}} .\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the integral on the right since it's the integral of a power function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{3}\\,\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the last step, we change the variable back to $x$ by replacing $u=x^2+1.$ So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int 2x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x \n=\\dfrac{(x^2+1)^4}{4}+C.\n$$\n\n**Note:** After solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{4}}{4}+C] & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[({x}^{2}+1)^{4}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4({x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+1)+0 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2x({x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070015 "How do we calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\,\\textrm d x?$\n\nIn this case, the numerator is the derivative of the denominator! Again, integrals like this are always solvable by substitution. So, we let\n\n$$\n\\color{red}u = x^2+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad {\\color{blue}{\\textrm d u = 2x \\, \\textrm d x}},\n$$\n\nand we can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\operatorname{ln}|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}|1+{x}^{2}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}(1+{x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\ln(1+x^2)+C.\n$$\n\nNotice that we removed the absolute value bars because the function $1+x^2$ is always positive." #edb/ref 13194139533438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070016 "We know how to integrate $\\cos{x}.$ But how do we integrate $\\cos{3x}?$ \n\nTo compute\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos{3x} \\, \\textrm dx,\n$$\n\nwe can use substitution. If we let $u=3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** After solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+C) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139534625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070017 "Suppose that we want to calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 2x \\cos{(x^2)}\\,\\textrm d x.$\n\nNotice that if we let $u = x^2,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{2x}_{u'} \\cos({\\overbrace{x^2}^u})\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the factor multiplying the trigonometric function is the derivative of the argument! Integrals like this can always be solved using substitution.\n\nSo, let $u=x^2.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d u = 2x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139534625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070018 "Suppose that we wish to solve the compound inequality \n\n$$\n4 \\le x + 2 \\le 8.\n$$\n\nTo solve the compound inequality, we apply the usual operations to the middle of the inequality to isolate $x.$ Keep in mind that whatever we do to the middle, we also need to do to *both* the left-hand *and* right-hand sides.\n\nHere, we can isolate $x$ by subtracting $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t4 & \\le x + 2 \\le 8 \\[5pt]\n\t4 \\,{\\color{red}-}\\, {\\color{red}{2}} &\\le x + 2 \\,{\\color{red}-}\\, {\\color{red}{2}} \\le 8 \\,{\\color{red}-}\\, {\\color{red}{2}} \\[5pt]\n\t2 &\\le x \\le 6\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $2 \\le x \\le 6.$" #edb/ref 13194139533438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070019 "When an equation contains an $x^2$ term and a constant, it can be solved using the **square root method**. The square root method makes use of the fact that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\vert x \\vert \\,.\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, we can solve the equation $2x^2-18=0$ using the following three steps:\n\n1\\. Isolate the $x^2$ on one side of the equation:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Take the square root of both sides of the equation\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |x| & =3\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}x & =3\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-3\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\nSo, there are two solutions, $x=3$ and $x=-3.$ We can write this more concisely as $x= \\pm 3,$ where the \"$\\pm$\" symbol stands for \"plus or minus.\"" #edb/ref 13194139535256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070020 "The **point-slope form** of a line that passes through any point $(x_1,y_1)$ with slope $m$ is given by \n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m(x-x_1).\n$$\n\n \n\nFor example, consider the straight line graph which passes through the points $(3,1)$ and $(5,5)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7838ffcb8f56ebffd384763ff761990e15031bdc5cdf3f0cc1f964ed69a4806.png)\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(3,1),$ so we can use $(x_1,y_1) = (3,1)$ in our point-slope formula. All that remains is to compute $m,$ and we can do this using the given points.\n\nFirst, calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{5-1}{5-3}=\\frac{4}{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $m=2$ and the point $(x_1,y_1) = (3,1)$ into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =2(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** If we simplify this point-slope formula, we obtain the slope-intercept form of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =2(x-3) \\\\ y-1 & =2x-6 \\\\ y & =2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** We could put $(x_1,y_1)=(5,5).$ In this case, the point-slope form will be different:\n\n$$\ny-5=2(x-5)\n$$\n\n \nBut the resulting simplified slope-intercept form will be the same:\n\n$$\ny = 2x-5\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070021 "Suppose we want to find the point of intersection between the two lines $y = x + 2$ and $y = 2x + 1.$ A sketch of both lines and the point of intersection $P$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/2b/2bf429fdfbe706521b81617f56a0f561171aa7fd48e0af89318225f1d328e667.png)\n\nThe point $P$ consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=x+2$ and $y=2x+1.$ So, to find the coordinates of $P$ we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=x+2 \\\\ y &=2x+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=x+2$ into the second equation $y=2x+1$ gives an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =2x+1 \\\\ x+1 & =2x \\\\ 1 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x = 1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=x+2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1, \\, y=3.$ So, the point of intersection has the coordinates $(1,3).$" #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186070022 "A **function** is a rule that takes a number as **input** and then **outputs** another number.\n\nFor instance, let's look at the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x) = 3x+1\n$$\n\nIn this example,\n\n- $f$ is the **name** of the function,\n\n- the number $x$ is the **input**,\n\n- the number $3x+1$ is the **output**.\n\nIf we **substitute** a number into our function in place of $x$, we get another number as output. \n\nFor example, by substituting $x={\\color{blue}{2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nf({\\color{blue}{2}}) = 3\\cdot {\\color{blue}{2}}+1 = 7.\n$$\n\nSo, the input $x={\\color{blue}{2}}$ results in an output of $7.$\n\nWe can substitute any number we like into this function! For example, if we substitute $x={\\color{red}{-2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nf({\\color{red}{-2}}) = 3\\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}})+1 = -5.\n$$\n\nSo, the input $x={\\color{red}{-2}}$ results in an output of $-5.$" #edb/ref 13194139535226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070023 "Functions with more than one input are called **multivariable functions**. \n\nFor example, the multivariable function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x-y\n$$\n\ntakes in two numbers, $x$ and $y$, and returns their difference. \n\nFor multivariable functions, the order of the inputs matters. For example, if we substitute $x={\\color{blue}{3}}$ and $y={\\color{red}{2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nf({\\color{blue}{3}},{\\color{red}{2}}) = {\\color{blue}{3}}-{\\color{red}{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nwhereas if we substitute $x={\\color{red}{2}}$ and $y={\\color{blue}{3}},$ we get a different output:\n\n$$\nf({\\color{red}{2}},{\\color{blue}{3}})={\\color{red}{2}}-{\\color{blue}{3}}=-1\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070024 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to solve equations that contain absolute value expressions.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following equation:\n\n$$\n|x+1| = 4\n$$\n\nFor this equation to be true, either the *positive or negative* of the expression inside the absolute value must equal the right-hand side. That is,\n\n$$\nx+1 = 4 \\qquad\\qquad \\text{ or } \\qquad\\qquad -(x+1)=4.\n$$\n\nSo, let's solve these two equations:\n\n- First, we solve the equation $x+1 = 4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =4 \\\\ x+1-1 & =4-1 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve the equation $-(x+1) = 4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(x+1) & =4 \\\\ -x-1 & =4 \\\\ -x-1+1 & =4+1 \\\\ -x & =5 \\\\ x & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original absolute value equation are $x={\\color{blue}3}$ and $x={\\color{red}{-5}}.$" #edb/ref 13194139535256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070025 "So, we've seen that the solutions to the equation \n\n$$\n|x+1| = 4\n$$\n\nare $x={\\color{blue}3}$ and $x={\\color{red}{-5}}.$ \n\nWe can verify that these solutions are correct by substituting them back into the original equation and checking we get a true statement:\n\n- Substituting $x={\\color{blue}3}$ into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+1| & =4 \\\\ |3+1| & =4 \\\\ |4| & =4 \\\\ 4 & =4.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x={\\color{blue}3}$ is indeed a solution to the absolute value equation.\n\n- Substituting $x={\\color{red}-5}$ into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+1| & =4 \\\\ |-5+1| & =4 \\\\ |-4| & =4 \\\\ 4 & =4.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x={\\color{red}-5}$ is indeed a solution to the absolute value equation.\n\nTherefore, $x={\\color{blue}3}$ and $x={\\color{red}{-5}}$ are both solutions to our absolute value equation." #edb/ref 13194139535256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070026 "**Absolute value inequalities** combine two basic concepts: absolute value and inequality. The absolute value of a number is its distance from zero, while an inequality specifies a range of values.\n\nConsider a simple absolute value inequality, as follows:\n\n$$\n|x| < 7\n$$\n\nThis inequality specifies every number $x$ that is less than a distance of $7$ from $0$. So, it represents the same thing as the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-7 < x < 7\n$$\n\nTo solve an absolute value inequality, we first convert it to a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. Then, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\nTo illustrate, let's solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n|2x+3| \\leq 5\n$$\n\nFirst, we interpret the inequality: the inequality specifies every number $2x+3$ whose distance from zero is less than or equal to $5.$ So, it represents the same thing as the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-5 \\leq 2x+3 \\leq 5\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\le 2x+3\\le 5 \\\\ -8\\le 2x\\le 2 \\\\ -4\\le x\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070027 "We take a similar approach for absolute value inequalities involving the \"greater than\" symbol. To illustrate, consider the following absolute value inequality:\n\n$$\n|x| \\geq 6\n$$\n\nTo solve this absolute value inequality, we convert it into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. The inequality specifies every number $x$ whose distance from zero is greater than or equal to $6.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\le -6 & \\text{or} &  & x & \\ge 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use the word \"or\" instead of \"and\" since only one of the inequalities $x\\leq-6$ or $x\\geq 6$ needs to be true for $|x| \\geq 6$ to be true." #edb/ref 13194139535523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070028 "What is the value of $x$ that satisfies the following equation?\n\n$$\n3^x = 3^4\n$$\n\nWe call this an **exponential equation** because the variable is contained within the exponent. \n\nNow, this equation is pretty easy to solve. Because the bases on both sides of the equation are the same (they are both equal to $3$), we can simply equate the powers.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x} & ={3}^{4} \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to this equation is $x=4.$" #edb/ref 13194139535264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070029 "Let's take a look at the following equation: \n\n$$\n5^x = 25\n$$\n\nWe can't solve this equation straight off the bat because the two sides of the equation don't share a common base.\n\nHowever, notice that $25$ can be written as a power of $5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n25=5^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite the equation as\n\n$$\n5^x = 5^2.\n$$\n\nSince both sides of the equation are now written as an exponential expression with a common base of $5,$ we can set the exponents equal to each other and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check that the solution is correct, we plug $x=2$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t5^{2} &= 25 \\\n\t\t25 &= 25 \\; {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070030 "Consider the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2+2x+5}\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral doesn't resemble any of the standard integrals we have encountered so far.\n\nHowever, notice that we can complete the square in the denominator as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+5 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+({1}^{2}-{1}^{2})+5 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{1}^{2})-{1}^{2}+5 \\\\  & =(x+1)^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug the above into our integral and collect a factor of ${\\color{red}{4}}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{4[(x+1)^{2}/4+1]}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}/4+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x+1)^{2}/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x+1)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this now resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+u^2} \\, \\textrm{d}u = \\arctan u + C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac{x+1}{2}\\right).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{x+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = 2\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x+1)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{x+1}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!" #edb/ref 13194139533439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070031 "We know that we can use the method of integration by parts to evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{4x} \\textrm{d}x.$ Now, suppose that we want to calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2e^{4x} \\textrm{d}x.$ Can we use integration by parts in this case?\n\nLet's  try. We define $u$ and $v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {e}^{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for integration by parts leads to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})-\\int (\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe still don't have an answer, but the problem is reduced to the calculation of a simpler integral  $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{4x} \\textrm{d}x.$ To compute this integral, we just need to apply the integration by parts formula again. For this second integral, we take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {e}^{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})-\\int (\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})+{C}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{16}\\,{e}^{4x}+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is a constant of integration.\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{16}\\,{e}^{4x}+{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{8}\\,x{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{32}\\,{e}^{4x}-\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\,{e}^{4x}(8{x}^{2}-4x+1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -\\dfrac{C_1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070032 "In some cases, applying integration by parts can lead to a cyclic pattern, where the original integral reappears after a few steps. This cyclic behavior allows us to solve the integral by forming an equation that can be solved algebraically.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following example:\n\n$$\nI = \\int \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x} \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's try using the method of integration by parts. We suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)(\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}⁡x-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}⁡x-I\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the same integral $I$ on both sides of the equality. Thinking about $I$ as some unknown variable, we can easily solve this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{2}⁡x-I \\\\ I+I & ={ln}^{2}⁡x \\\\ 2I & ={ln}^{2}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we added a constant of integration right at the very end of the calculation." #edb/ref 13194139535953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070033 "To find the distance between two points on the Cartesian plane, we can create a right triangle and use the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the points $(2, 3)$ and $(5, 7)$, shown in the diagram below. We draw a right triangle so that the segment between the two points is the hypotenuse.\n\n![](images/3d/3d3629fbd659ebfb6d32080feeb64bd2ebdd3fb63e9826970d64aa41a00b9a56.png)\n\nWe can find the distance between the points by finding the length of the hypotenuse $d$ using the Pythagorean theorem in the form\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}\n$$\n\nFrom this perspective:\n\n- $a$ is the distance between the $x$-coordinates.\n$a|= x_{2} - x_{1}; = 5 - 2; = 3$\n- $b$ is the distance between the $y$-coordinates.\n$b|= y_{2} - y_{1}; = 7 - 3; = 4$\n\nSo the distance between the points $(2, 3)$ and $(5, 7)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} d &= \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{9 + 16} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{25} \\\\[3pt] &= 5. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, the distance $d$ between any two points in the Cartesian plane is given by the **distance formula**:\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070034 "When two lines are given in the *standard form*, you may find it easier to use the *elimination method* to find the point of intersection.\n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 13194139533349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070035 "How do we evaluate an integral like $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)} \\textrm{d}x?$\n\nFirst of all, note that the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions:\n \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)}=\\dfrac{A}{(x+2)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x-3)}\n$$\n\nIf we can figure out the partial fractions, then we can integrate each of the partial fractions using the natural logarithm.\n\nSo, let's compute the partial fractions. If we multiply the above equation by $(x+2)(x-3),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =A(x-3)+B(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression above must hold for all values of $x.$ Plugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-2)-1 & =A(-2-3)+B(-2+2) \\\\ -5 & =A(-5)+B(0) \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)-1 & =A(3-3)+B(3+2) \\\\ 5 & =A(0)+B(5) \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)} = \\dfrac{1}{(x+2)} +\\dfrac{1}{(x-3)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can integrate each of the partial fractions separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x+2}+\\frac{1}{x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}|x+2|+\\operatorname{ln}|x-3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!" #edb/ref 13194139533441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070036 "Suppose we are given the integral  $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2} \\textrm{d}x.$ Note that we have the repeated factor $(x+1)$ in the denominator. \n\nThe correct form of the partial fraction expansion of the integrand is\n \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2}=\\dfrac{A}{(x+1)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x+1)^2} \\,.\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides the above equation by $(x+1)^2$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =A(x+1)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-1)+2 & =A(-1+1)+B \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+2 & =A(0+1)+B \\\\ 2 & =A+B \\\\ 2 & =A-1 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2}= \\dfrac{3}{(x+1)} -\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nNow this function can be integrated term by term. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+2}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{(x+1)}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}|x+1|+\\frac{1}{x+1}+C\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant of integration." #edb/ref 13194139533441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070037 "To solve a two-term quadratic equation like $x^2 - 7x = 0$ where there is no constant term, we can factor out the variable and apply the zero-product rule.\n\nFactoring out the variable, we have:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\tx^2 - 7x &= 0 \\\n\tx(x - 7) &= 0\n}\n$$\n\nWe apply the zero product rule to get \n\n$$\nx=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x-7=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives \n\n$$\nx=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=0,7.$\n\nTo check that these solutions are correct, we can substitute them back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =0 & \\, & \\, & x & =7 \\\\  & ⇓ &  &  &  & ⇓ \\\\ {x}^{2}-7x & =0 &  &  & {x}^{2}-7x & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}-7(0) & =0 &  &  & {7}^{2}-7(7) & =0 \\\\ 0-0 & =0 &  &  & 49-49 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓ &  &  & 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Warning:** It's tempting to solve the equation by dividing by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-7x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-7x}{x} & =\\frac{0}{x} \\\\ x-7 & =0 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we do that, we lose the solution $x=0.$ So, when solving equations, *never divide by a variable* because the variable could be zero, and division by zero is not allowed. Instead, try to factor the variable in the equation." #edb/ref 13194139535822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070038 "To perform partial fraction decomposition, we always need to factor the denominator as much as possible before using the factors to write the partial fractions.\n\nSometimes, however, we get **irreducible quadratic factors** that cannot be written as a product of linear factors with real coefficients. For example, the factor $x^2+4$ is irreducible.\n\nRemember that in the partial fraction corresponding to an irreducible quadratic factor, the numerator is a linear expression. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+4)}+\\frac{C}{(x-1)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general:\n\n- Any expression of the form $x^2+c,$ where $c>0,$ is irreducible.\n\n- Any expression of the form $ax^2 + bx + c$ is irreducible if the discriminant $\\mathcal{D}$ is negative, i.e., if $\\mathcal{D} = b^2 - 4ac < 0.$\n\n- Any expression of the form $ax^2 + bx + c$ is irreducible (over the rational numbers) if the discriminant $\\mathcal{D} > 0$ is not a perfect square. For example, if $\\mathcal D = 5,$ then the quadratic term does not factor over the rational numbers because $\\sqrt{\\mathcal{D}} = \\sqrt 5$ is not a rational number." #edb/ref 13194139533441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070039 "If we want to plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=-\\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\n, we reflect the curve\n\n$$\ny= \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nin the $x$-axis, as shown below:\n\n![](images/70/7015bb46abb6bf678efd79c9e8005975d532d795275b4af859fac385cdf60348.png)\n\nThis transformation is called a **reflection in the $x$-axis**, and it occurs because the output $y$-values of the function\n\n$$\ny= \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nare made negative by the transformation.\n\nIf we combine vertical and horizontal translations with reflection, we get a general form\n\n$$\ny=-\\mid x-h \\mid +k\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a reflected absolute value graph\n\n$$\ny=-\\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nthat is translated\n\n- $h$ units right and\n- $k$ units up." #edb/ref 13194139533349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070040 "Suppose that we want to find the finite area bounded between the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=1.$ \n\n![](images/af/af462c91b8348f2daf5c38a7e705bad3b055919269114e80608a5aa0536a7b01.png)\n\nThe area $A$ of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{-1}^{1} \\left[\\, \\left(\\color{brown}{\\textrm{upper function}}\\right) - \\left(\\color{blue}{\\textrm{lower function}}\\right) \\,\\right] \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo in our case, the area is expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{-1}^{1} \\big[ ({\\color{brown}{1}}) - ({\\color{blue}{x^2}}) \\big]\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-1}^{1} (1-x^2)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow that the integral has been written down correctly, we can evaluate it using the usual techniques:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[1-\\frac{1}{3}]-[-1-\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-[-\\frac{2}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the area of the region is $\\dfrac{4}{3}.$\n\nLastly, let's recap a few things:\n\n- The limits of integration correspond to the $x$-values at the points of intersection of the two functions.\n\n- If you're not sure which is the lower function and which is the upper, one way to find out is to draw a vertical line that *points upward* through the region, like so:\n\n![](images/00/00a80b851fbb08d25a110c8532606830271744e9ccb1377a43949def5af9f308.png)\n\nThe vertical line always \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=1.$" #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070041 "Lastly, let's discuss why the area formula works.\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{a}^{b}\\left(\\color{brown}{\\textrm{upper function}}\\right) - \\left(\\color{blue}{\\textrm{lower function}}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nConsider the area bounded by the two functions $y = f(x)$ and $y = g(x).$ We'll assume that the two curves intersect at $x=\\pm 1,$ and for $x\\in(-1,1)$ we have $f(x) > g(x),$ so $f(x)$ is the upper function.\n\nWe can approximate the area using rectangles in the following way. First, we will divide the interval $[-1,1]$ into small subintervals of equal length $\\Delta{x}.$ One such subinterval is shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/4c/4c8c171e7b57a566fe7372248ad6e77d6a756e91cfc1a164a0d60fa042a38462.png)\n\nThe area $\\Delta{A}$ bounded between the curves $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ can be approximated by the area of the rectangle shown below, with the sides $\\Delta{x}$ (horizontal) and $f(x)-g(x)$ (vertical),\n\n$$\n\\Delta{A} \\approx \\left(f(x)-g(x)\\right)\\Delta{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/20/209ddeac16dc7a83b49f871adfaf81ef78296009ea2fb0ffc8e7d2d4554e0a55.png)\n\nThe sum over all the rectangles will approximate the total area $A$ that we want. This approximation becomes more accurate as the rectangles get narrower, i.e., $\\Delta{x} \\to 0.$ And the limit of the sum, as $\\Delta x \\to 0,$ is the required integral!\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A={\\int }_{a}^{b}(f(x)-g(x))\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070042 "Suppose we want to find the area bounded between the curves\n\n$$\nf(y)=3-y^2\n$$\n\nand $g(y)=-1$.\n\n![](images/ce/ce89ba9ff82cff47efefd76a78f1d85636b8ddf4e9c5b5aa7046b4a8d99d834c.png)\n\nWe can adopt the same approach that we used for functions of $x$. The only difference is that now we're working with functions of $y$. So we write the integral in terms of $y$, and we subtract the left curve from the right curve.\n\nSo, the area $A$ of the region is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\int_{c}^{d}[(right function) - (left function)]dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find for which values of $y$ the curves intersect. In our case, the intersections of the curves are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(y) &= g(y) \\\\\n3 - y^{2} &= -1 \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4 \\\\\ny &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe right function is\n\n$$\nf(y) = 3-y^2\n$$\n\nand the left function is $g(y)=-1$. So the area that we want can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\int_{-2}^{2}(f(y) - g(y))dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{-2}^{2}((3 - y^{2}) - (-1))dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{-2}^{2}(4 - y^{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we get\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{32}{3}\n$$\n\n**Note:** If you're not sure which is the right function and which is the left, one way to find out is to consider some $y$ between the bounds $y=-2$ and $y=2$, and draw a horizontal line that passes through $y$ and points in the positive direction along the $x$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ce/ce29a8ab25fc443cabd7100c7b20feca95885b98699a11fde6aa5bb42e85991b.png)\n\nThe horizontal line always *enters* the region through the left function and *leaves* through the right function." #edb/ref 13194139533349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074926 "We'll now discuss how to prove the exterior angle theorem. Let's begin by stating this:\n\n> *An exterior angle in a triangle equals the sum of the measures of the two non-adjacent interior angles.*\n\nConsider the diagram below, where the points $F$, $H$, and $I$ are colinear.\n\n![](images/02/02c5f332f4a3d89f9d674ba8afe1867e607b09190346ff781e2bfedf03a0ce91.png)\n\nWe wish to prove the following statement:\n\n> $m\\angle{IHG} = m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}$\n\nSeveral different proofs of this result are available, and we'll study two of them." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074927 "Let's now state our three properties of equality more generally:\n\n- The **reflexive** property of equality:\n$a = a$\nThis states that every number equals itself.\n- The **symmetric** property of equality:\n$\\textrm{If}\\: a = b, \\textrm{then}\\: b=a$\nThis states that if the first number equals the second, the second must equal the first.\n- The **transitive** property of equality:\n$\\textrm{If}\\: a = b \\:\\textrm{and}\\: b=c,\\textrm{then}\\:a=c$\nThis states that if the first number equals the second and the second equals the third, the first must equal the third." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074928 "![](images/20/205847b29a751ab521823089d7386d8ce26d2326f788cdaf44a5e7d90c12e408.png)\n\nCongruent *angles* also have the properties of reflexivity, symmetricity, and transitivity. Let's list them:\n\n- The **reflexive** property of congruence:\n$\\angle a \\cong \\angle a$\nThis states that every angle is congruent to itself.\n- The **symmetric** property of congruence:\n$\\textrm{If}\\: \\angle a \\cong \\angle b, \\textrm{then}\\: \\angle b \\cong \\angle a$\nThis states that if the first angle is congruent to the second, the second must be congruent to the first.\n- The **transitive** property of congruence:\n$\\textrm{If}\\: \\angle a \\cong \\angle b \\:\\textrm{and}\\: \\angle b \\cong \\angle c,\\textrm{then}\\:\\angle a \\cong \\angle c$\nThis states that if the first angle is congruent to the second, and the second is congruent to the third, the first must be congruent to the third." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074929 "Suppose that $a, b$, and $c$ are all segments. Let's state our three properties of segment congruence more generally.\n\n- The *reflexive* property of congruence:\n$a \\cong a$\nThis states that every segment is congruent to itself.\n- The *symmetric* property of congruence:\n$\\textrm{If}\\: a \\cong b, \\textrm{then}\\: b \\cong a$\nThis states that if the first segment is congruent to the second, the second must be congruent to the first.\n- The *transitive* property of congruence:\n$\\textrm{If}\\: a \\cong b \\:\\textrm{and}\\: b \\cong c,\\textrm{then}\\:a \\cong c$\nThis states that if the first segment is congruent to the second, and the second is congruent to the third, the first must be congruent to the third." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074930 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to find a point that partitions a line segment in a given ratio. We'll start by considering horizontal and vertical lines in the plane before generalizing to any two-dimensional segment.\n\nConsider the following horizontal segment in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/8a/8a52305a4a3ca3e4ecb87a4b340b4d4048e70e59d7018a22334f397e17fc4355.png)\n\nSuppose we want to find the point $C$ on this segment that divides it in the ratio $1:6$.\n\n![](images/bf/bfaa641cc65e887af7e3573195e7915f0043117b8603afb8b8269368e5890be0.png)\n\nFirst, notice that the points $A$ and $B$ have $y=0$. Consequently, the point $C$ also has $y=0$.\n\nPartitioning a line segment $\\overline{AB}$ in the ratio $a:b$ means that we must\n\n- divide the line segment into $a+b$ equal parts, and\n- find the point $C$ that is $a$ equal parts from $A$.\n\nIn our case, the ratio $1:6$ implies $AC: CB=1:6$. This means\n\n- there are $1+6 = 7$ equal parts, and\n- we want the point that is $1$ equal part from $A$. So, we have\n$AC= \\dfrac{1}{7}AB$\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ee/ee2fa7322e2880cccb3b1dce2877d1762e7710cd5533b0c834d60a726965b498.png)\n\nNow, since $\\overline{AB}$ is horizontal, its length is simply the difference between the $x$-coordinates of the endpoints:\n\n$$\nAB = x_B - x_A\n$$\n\nLet's add this to our diagram.\n\n![](images/f8/f80472e71930cb70b95f692f243b9a1144bc16631cb3043fffd9ede307688912.png)\n\nFinally, to find the coordinates of $C$, we start at the point $A$ and \"run\" from $A$ to $C$ horizontally. So, the $x$-coordinate of $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{C} &= x_{A} + \\frac{1}{7}(x_{B} - x_{A}) \\\\\n&= 1 + \\frac{1}{7}(8 - 1) \\\\\n&= 1 + 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C$ has coordinates $(2,0)$." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074931 "We can also partition a *vertical* segment in a given ratio.\n\nSuppose we want to find a point $C$ on the line segment from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(0,17)$ that divides the segment in the ratio $7:1$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/37/375094ce80e9ecf26af920ffd296683074d17137009a09e8016ded92565fcd60.png)\n\nFirst, notice that the $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$ are the same. Therefore, the points $A$ and $B$ lie on the vertical line $x=0$. Consequently, the point $C$ also has $x=0$.\n\nPartitioning a line segment $\\overline{AB}$ into a ratio $a:b$ means we must\n\n- divide the segment into $a+b$ equal parts, and\n- find the point $C$ that is $a$ equal parts from $A$.\n\nIn our case, the ratio $7:1$ implies $AC:CB=7:1$. This means\n\n- there are $7+1 = 8$ equal parts, and\n- we want to find the point $C$ that is $7$ equal parts from $A$. So, we have\n$AC= \\dfrac{7}{8}AB$\nas shown below.![](images/8a/8a812f3a9bd772fd5965a6bd29defd4e7fbfec11721da878ce6ac0c864bf09d3.png)\n\nNow, since $AB$ is vertical, its length is simply the difference between the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints:\n\n$$\nAB = y_B - y_A\n$$\n\nLet's add this to our diagram.\n\n![](images/70/70d275039d865198b7bb05a421bbad4a6942214dc2e63b81a2993175446c9172.png)\n\nFinally, to get the coordinates of $C$, we start at the point $A$ and \"rise\" vertically from $A$ to $C$. So, the $y$-coordinate of $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{C} &= y_{A} + \\frac{7}{8}(y_{B} - y_{A}) \\\\\n&= 1 + \\frac{7}{8}(17 - 1) \\\\\n&= 1 + 14 \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C$ has coordinates $(0,15)$." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074932 "We now move on to the relationship between the *volumes* of similar shapes:\n\n> *If the lengths of a three-dimensional object are enlarged by a scale factor of $k$, then the volume of the new shape will be enlarged by a scale factor of $k^3$.*\n\nLet's consider our cubes once more.\n\n![](images/f2/f21dbd828dc52470593a42a1139758d3772fc152661a278c3fc688bdde41e4e1.png)\n\nThe total volume of the smaller cube $V_S$ is\n\n$$\nV_{S} = (1\\,\\textrm{cm})^3 = 1\\,\\textrm{cm}^3\n$$\n\nThe scale factor is\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{{\\color{red}{2}}\\,\\textrm{cm}}{{\\color{blue}{1}}\\,\\textrm{cm}} = 2\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $k=2$, we can compute the volume of the larger cube as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{L} &= k^{3} \\cdot V_{S} \\\\\n&= 2^{3} \\cdot 1cm^{3} \\\\\n&= 8 \\cdot 1cm^{3} \\\\\n&= 8cm^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify that this is correct by computing the volume of the larger cube directly.\n\n$$\nV_{L} = (2\\,\\textrm{cm})^3 = 8\\,\\textrm{cm}^3\n$$\n\nwhich agrees with our previous calculation." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074933 "Before we move on, we should note the following important results.\n\n- All spheres are similar.\n- By extension, all hemispheres are similar, all quarter-spheres are similar, and all one-eighth spheres are similar.\n- All cubes are similar." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074934 "![](images/ef/ef37966a1091cd7626d55305fb24f4ee88490a41746d8929ce7935071a82b902.png)\n\nWe wish to prove the following statement:\n\n> *The sum of the measures of the interior angles of $\\triangle{ABC}$ equals $180^\\circ$.*\n\nThe key to unlocking this proof is to *construct* a line parallel to $\\overline{AB}$ passing through $C$, and then apply the alternate interior angles theorem twice.\n\nWe'll now go through the step-by-step proof of this statement.\n\n- **Step 1**: We start by constructing a line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{DE}$ that passes through the point $C$ and is parallel to $\\overline{AB}$.\n\n![](images/72/72228072b08d97fec98735c251439585a5d897e635794ebb29e0974cb86d9f1f.png)\n\n- **Step 2**: Since $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{DE}$ are parallel, $\\angle{ACD}$ and $\\angle{A}$ are congruent by the *alternate interior angles theorem*.\n\n![](images/f6/f69a095ed1a97cfdc5e71135bc2045aaf29dc078600f59d00482f99967bde407.png)\n\n- **Step 3**: Since $\\angle{ACD}\\cong \\angle{A}$, we have\n$m\\angle{ACD} = m\\angle{A}$\nby the *definition of congruent angles.*\n- **Step 4**: Since $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{DE}$ are parallel, $\\angle{BCE}$ and $\\angle{B}$ are congruent by the *alternate interior angles theorem*.\n\n![](images/ba/bac625b7cbded8e9b02798cf3c68562af89367e258f41b736f175c29bb995010.png)\n\n- **Step 5**: Since $\\angle{BCE} \\cong \\angle{B}$, we have\n$m\\angle{BCE} = m\\angle{B}$\nby the *definition of congruent angles*.\n- **Step 6**: The segments $\\overline{AC}$ and $\\overline{BC}$ divide the straight angle $\\angle{DCE}$ into three consecutive angles: $\\angle{ACD}$, $\\angle{ACB}$, and $\\angle{BCE}$. So, by the *angle addition postulate*, we must have\n$m\\angle{ACD} + m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{BCE} = m\\angle{DCE}$.\n- **Step 7**: In steps 3 and 5, we showed that $m\\angle{ACD} = m\\angle{A}$ and $m\\angle{BCE} = m\\angle{B}$, respectively. So, by *substituting* these values into the equality in step 6, we obtain\n$m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{B} = m\\angle{DCE}$.\n- **Step 8**: Since $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{DE}$ is a line, $m\\angle{DCE} = 180^\\circ$ by the *definition of a straight angle*.\n- **Step 9**: In step 8, we showed that $m\\angle{DCE} = 180^\\circ$, and in step 7, we showed that\n$m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{B} = m\\angle{DCE}$.\nTherefore, by the *transitive property of equality*, we have\n$m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{B} = 180^\\circ$,\nas required.\n\nIt's **highly recommended** that you practice reconstructing this proof. Try to do this *without* referring to these notes." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074935 "![](images/72/72228072b08d97fec98735c251439585a5d897e635794ebb29e0974cb86d9f1f.png)\n\nThe table below shows the proof of the interior angles of a triangle theorem in a two-column format.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\overline{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{DE}$ | Geometric construction |\n| 2 | $\\angle{ACD} \\cong \\angle{A}$ | Alternate interior angles theorem |\n| 3 | $m\\angle{ACD} = m\\angle{A}$ | Definition of congruent angles |\n| 4 | $\\angle{BCE} \\cong \\angle{B}$ | Alternate interior angles theorem |\n| 5 | $m\\angle{BCE} = m\\angle{B}$ | Definition of congruent angles |\n| 6 | $m\\angle{ACD} + m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{BCE} = m\\angle{DCE}$ | Angle addition postulate |\n| 7 | $m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{B} = m\\angle{DCE}$ | Substitution |\n| 8 | $m\\angle{DCE}=180^\\circ$ | Definition of a straight angle |\n| 9 | $m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{B} = 180^\\circ$ | Transitive property of equality |\n\nLet's now consider an alternative proof of this theorem using the corresponding angles postulate and the vertical angles theorem." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074936 "![](images/8f/8f850ea0f0b0cdd54995561870cd8d896f290b9fd6b07de11ca0674c1a8a412b.png)\n\nOur goal is to prove the following:\n\n> $m\\angle{IHG} = m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}$\n\nTo prove this theorem, we construct a ray from the point $H$ that's parallel to $\\overline{FG}$, and then apply the corresponding angles postulate and alternate interior angles theorem.\n\nWe complete the proof as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: We start by constructing a line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{HJ}$ through the point $H$ and parallel to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{FG}$.![](images/ca/ca53ee7d8d2f6390e9aa8692bc0f1400aa78f84cf5afa0457b13f86f0921199e.png)\n- **Step 2**: Since $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{FG} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{HJ}$, we have that the corresponding angles $\\angle{IHJ}$ and $\\angle{F}$ are congruent by the *corresponding angles postulate*.Let's add this to our diagram.![](images/18/185672f4600e86a4e6e92e49d46cf966a46206128b4113ec4e070ff987cb5a7b.png)\n- **Step 3**: Since $\\angle{IHJ} \\cong \\angle{F}$ are congruent, we have\n$m\\angle{IHJ} = m\\angle{F}$\nby the *definition of congruent angles*.\n- **Step 4**: Since $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{FG}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{HJ}$ are parallel, we have that the alternate interior angles $\\angle{JHG}$ and $\\angle{G}$ are congruent by the *alternate interior angles theorem*.Let's add this to our diagram.![](images/f4/f441d13eb67dbbd6d301d479dd3ac0d6dd0ac79fd6fbc56c8fc90521f5c7cd24.png)\n- **Step 5**: Since $\\angle{JHG}$ and $\\angle{G}$ are congruent, we have\n$m\\angle{JHG} = m\\angle{G}$\nby the *definition of congruent angles*.\n- **Step 6**: In steps 3 and 5, we showed that $m\\angle{IHJ}=m\\angle{F}$ and $m\\angle{JHG}=m\\angle{G}$, respectively. So, by the *addition principle*, we get\n$m∠IHJ|= m∠F; m∠IHJ + m∠JHG|= m∠F + m∠JHG; m∠IHJ + m∠JHG|= m∠F + m∠G$.\n- **Step 7**: Notice that $\\overline{HJ}$ is the common side of the angles $\\angle{IHJ}$ and $\\angle{JHG}$. Therefore, by the *angle addition postulate*, we have that\n$m\\angle{IHG} = m\\angle{IHJ} + m\\angle{JHG}$.\n- **Step 8**: In steps 7 and 6, respectively, we showed that\n${\\color{blue}m\\angle{IHG}} = m\\angle{IHJ} + m\\angle{JHG}$,\nand\n$m\\angle{IHJ} + m\\angle{JHG} = {\\color{blue}m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}}$.\nTherefore, by the *transitive property of equality*, we get\n${\\color{blue}m\\angle{IHG}} = {\\color{blue}m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}}$,\nas required.\n\nAgain, it's **highly recommended** that you practice reconstructing this proof." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074937 "![](images/6d/6df87ea11d90f73a6d35f7a37d15f46e4f807cbdaaeda0714d7a92cfc6afde67.png)\n\nThe table below shows our proof of the exterior angle theorem in a two-column format.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{FG} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{HJ}$ | Given |\n| 2 | $\\angle{IHJ} \\cong \\angle{F}$ | Corresponding angles postulate |\n| 3 | $m\\angle{IHJ} = m\\angle{F}$ | Definition of congruent angles |\n| 4 | $\\angle{JHG} \\cong \\angle{G}$ | Alternate interior angles theorem |\n| 5 | $m\\angle{JHG} = m\\angle{G}$ | Definition of congruent angles |\n| 6 | $m\\angle{IHJ} + m\\angle{JHG} = m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}$ | Addition principle |\n| 7 | $m\\angle{IHG} = m\\angle{IHJ} + m\\angle{JHG}$ | Angle addition postulate |\n| 8 | $m\\angle{IHG} = m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}$ | Transitive property of equality |\n\nLet's now consider a proof of the same theorem that uses the interior angles of a triangle theorem." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074938 "![](images/51/510becfb1fe21822369dd65fb45168e9b9d08b076d9a51b712415b8243c61f7b.png)\n\nThe table below shows the proof of the isosceles triangle theorem in a two-column format.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\overline{AM}$ is a median of $\\triangle ABC$ | Geometric construction |\n| 2 | $\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$ | Given |\n| 3 | $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{BC}$ | Definition of median |\n| 4 | $BM = CM$ | Definition of a midpoint |\n| 5 | $\\overline{BM} \\cong \\overline{CM}$ | Definition of congruent segments |\n| 6 | $\\overline{AM} \\cong \\overline{AM}$ | Reflexive property of congruence |\n| 7 | $\\triangle ABM \\cong \\triangle ACM$ | SSS criterion |\n| 8 | $\\angle {B} \\cong \\angle {C}$ | CPCTC |" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074939 "We occasionally skip referencing the symmetric property when proving mathematical statements involving equality and congruence. This helps to make our proofs a bit shorter.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following two equations:\n\n$$\nx = y^2, \\qquad x = 2\n$$\n\nWe can construct a formal proof that $y^2 = 2$ using a so-called two-column format as follows:\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $x=y^2$ | Given |\n| 2 | $y^2 = x$ | Symmetric property of equality |\n| 3 | $x=2$ | Given |\n| 4 | $y^2=2$ | Transitive property of equality |\n\nThe last line is true because, from rows 2 and 3 respectively, we have\n\n$$\ny^2 = {\\color{red}{x}} \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad {\\color{red}{x}}=2\n$$\n\nand so by the transitive property of equality,\n$y^2 = 2$.\n\nWe can construct a shorter proof by omitting the second line:\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $x=y^2$ | Given |\n| 3 | $x=2$ | Given |\n| 3 | $y^2=2$ | Transitive property of equality |\n\nIn this proof, the symmetric property is still applied \"under the hood,\" but we don't explicitly refer to it." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074940 "Let's now prove that opposite angles in an inscribed quadrilateral are supplementary. Consider the following diagram.\n\n![](images/fc/fc5d8e9aa36a090c178f1e786da87dde2fb6f2ae8e009035606a8d4575e77abf.png)\n\nBy the definition of supplementary angles, we must prove the relation\n\n$$\nm\\angle A + m\\angle C = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nLet's draw the radii $\\overline{OD}$ and $\\overline{OB}$.\nWe call $\\gamma$ the measure of the angle $\\angle DOB$, as shown in the diagram. Notice that $\\angle DOB$ is a central angle of the intercepted arc $\\overset{\\frown}{DB}$ (the arc in red). Moreover, $\\angle C$ is an inscribed angle on the same arc.\n\n![](images/d5/d5daf375642bb5b474daca0ffe04eec95b13e9e79d676f8d6f411a36ad70b5da.png)\n\n$$\n\\gamma\n$$\n\n$$\nm\\angle C{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\gamma = 2m\\angle C\n$$\n\nNow, we call $\\alpha$ the measure of the reflex angle $\\angle DOB$.\n\n![](images/42/426ef430dbc0bcba86b95600a3e5c0591a985c62215a249836694381a95b2fb7.png)\n\n$$\n\\alpha\n$$\n\n$$\n\\angle A\n$$\n\n$$\n\\angle A\n$$\n\n$$\n\\angle DOB{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 2m\\angle A\n$$\n\nNotice that the angle $\\angle DOB$ and the reflex $\\angle DOB$ make a full turn.\n\n![](images/92/92118df41719c096ff484464b9d4f63320303ff01b91aa3bed74819c3625a162.png)\n\n$$\n360^\\circ{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\alpha + \\gamma= 360^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the relations\n\n$$\n\\gamma = 2m\\angle C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 2m\\angle A\n$$\n\ninto the above, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nα + γ &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n2m∠A + 2m∠C &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n2(m∠A + m∠C) &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nm∠A + m∠C &= 180^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074941 "Let's now prove the inscribed angle theorem.\n\nConsider the following diagram.\n\n![](images/a8/a801330cb46310e8e1b5d2548a29f455e5ced3cf75b5409ea9c14ea87096cd7d.png)\n\nWe want to prove the relation\n\n$$\nm\\angle BOA = 2m\\angle BPA\n$$\n\nThe radius $\\overline{OP}$ splits $\\angle BPA$ into the consecutive angles $\\angle APO$ and $\\angle BPO$. Let's call the measure of these angles\n\n$$\nm\\angle APO = \\alpha\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nm\\angle BPO= \\beta\n$$\n\n![](images/4a/4a7bd699aa77636435da7850b539ecc01ede9dca5a05d74af8f342d9d33653e0.png)\n\n$$\n\\triangle APO\n$$\n\n$$\n\\overline{AO}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\overline{PO}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\triangle APO\n$$\n\n$$\nm\\angle OAP = m\\angle APO = \\alpha\n$$\n\n$$\n\\triangle BPO\n$$\n\n$$\nm\\angle OBP = m\\angle BPO = \\beta\n$$\n\n![](images/03/035c692443ec4764b2d5d7b8ac3490d8d92a624bb7cdbeed48c187d9862c040d.png)\n\nSince the sum of the interior angles of a triangle equals $180^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n2\\alpha + m\\angle POA = 180^\\circ\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad m\\angle POA = 180^\\circ - 2\\alpha\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n2\\beta + m\\angle POB = 180^\\circ \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad m\\angle POB = 180^\\circ - 2\\beta\n$$\n\nNotice that the arcs $\\overset{\\frown}{PA}$, $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\frown}{BP}$ partition the circle. Therefore, the sum of their angles must be a full turn:\n\n$$\nm \\angle BOA + m\\angle POA + m\\angle POB = 360^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe substitute our expressions for $m\\angle POA$ and $m\\angle POB$ into the above and simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BOA + m∠POA + m∠POB &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nm∠BOA + (180^{∘} - 2α) + (180^{∘} - 2β) &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nm∠BOA - 2α - 2β &= 0 \\\\\nm∠BOA &= 2(α + β)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BOA &= 2(α + β) \\\\\nm∠BOA &= 2(m∠OAP + m∠OBP) \\\\\nm∠BOA &= 2m∠BPA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074942 "When we compare two ratios, we are comparing how much one quantity is associated with another quantity.\n\nOne way to compare ratios is to write each ratio as a fraction and simplify.\n\nFor example, suppose Store A has $12$ pens and $3$ notebooks, and Store B has $15$ pens and $5$ notebooks.\n\nThe ratio of pens to notebooks at Store A is $12:3$, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12 \\div 3}{3 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{4}{1} = 4\n$$\n\nSo the ratio $12:3$ is equivalent to $4:1$.\n\nThe ratio of pens to notebooks at Store B is $15:5$, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing the numerator and denominator by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15 \\div 5}{5 \\div 5} = \\dfrac{3}{1} = 3\n$$\n\nSo the ratio $15:5$ is equivalent to $3:1$.\n\nSince $4 > 3$, Store A has a larger ratio of pens to notebooks than Store B." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074943 "When comparing two ratios, we can sometimes use\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nas a benchmark to compare the ratios as fractions.\n\nFirst, we recall the following facts:\n\n- If the numerator of a fraction is less than half of the denominator, then the fraction is less than $\\dfrac12$.\n- On the other hand, if the numerator is greater than half of the denominator, then a fraction is greater than $\\dfrac12$.\n\nIf one fraction is less than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nand the other fraction is greater than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nthen the fraction less than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nis always the smaller fraction.\n\nLet's apply this to a concrete example." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074944 "Sometimes it is helpful to compare two ratios by writing them as fractions.\n\nTo do this, write each ratio as a fraction, simplify, and then compare the simplified fractions.\n\nFor example, suppose Jar A has $3$ blue marbles and $24$ red marbles, and Jar B has $5$ blue marbles and $20$ red marbles.\n\nThe ratio of blue marbles to red marbles in Jar A is $3:24$, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{24}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\div 3}{24 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nThe ratio of blue marbles to red marbles in Jar B is $5:20$, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5 \\div 5}{20 \\div 5} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTo compare\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nwe look at the denominators. Since $8$ is larger than $4$, the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nrepresents a smaller portion.\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8} < \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nJar A has a smaller ratio of blue marbles to red marbles than Jar B." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074945 "Let's compare the following two fractions. Which of them is larger?\n\n$$\n\\dfrac27, \\quad \\dfrac45\n$$\n\nFirst, we compare each to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\n- To compare $\\dfrac27$ to $\\dfrac12$, we check whether $2$ is less than half of $7$.\nSince half of $7$ is $3.5$, we have $2 < 3.5$, so\n$\\dfrac27 < \\dfrac12$.\n- Similarly, to compare $\\dfrac45$ to $\\dfrac12$, we check whether $4$ is more than half of $5$.\nSince half of $5$ is $2.5$, we have $4 > 2.5$, so\n$\\dfrac45 > \\dfrac12$.\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac27\n$$\n\nis less than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac45\n$$\n\nis greater than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nwe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac27 < \\dfrac45\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac27\n$$\n\nrepresents the *smaller* ratio.\n\n![](images/90/90400dbadef48a4e9f929152e24e050d2d233f177f84c7bbc253db952192b0df.png)\n\nLet's now apply this to a real-world example involving ratios." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074946 "Graphs can help us solve word problems involving two quantities.\n\nThe graph below shows the relationship between the number of notebooks purchased and the total cost.\n\n- The horizontal axis shows the number of notebooks $n$.\n- The vertical axis shows the total cost $c$ (in dollars).\n\n![](images/50/50b44dded1abdb0e3d2e48f050552b95016e305a59be33766f2319bae2b619c8.png)\n\nThe graph contains many points. Each point on the graph matches a value of $n$ with its corresponding value of $c$.\n\nSuppose we want to find the total cost when $2$ notebooks are purchased. To do so, we:\n\n- find $n={\\color{blue}2}$ on the *horizontal* axis,\n- move *vertically* from this point until we reach the graph, and\n- move *horizontally left* from the graph, and read off the $c$-value.\n\n![](images/1f/1fc01530168d813156668b5fb11542cebc0f9c40006d0f398db82a9931cb3f95.png)\n\nThe corresponding value on the vertical axis is\n\n$$\nc = {\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cost for $2$ notebooks is $\\[math]5$.\n\nWhen reading a graph, be careful to match each axis to the correct quantity. This is because, if we are given a value on the *vertical* axis, we must work in the other direction to find the corresponding value on the *horizontal* axis.\n\nLet's see an example of this in the next slide." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074947 "Sometimes we are given a graph and asked to find a value that is not shown directly on the graph. To solve this problem, we must first determine the equation of the graph.\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose a parking garage charges a flat entry fee plus a cost per hour.\nThe total cost $c$ (in dollars) after $h$ hours is shown in the graph below.\n\n![](images/56/56c832967f1a2cf782dcb0d47b1e968c074b30ea1fb9ce2c8dffc0cefadb161a.png)\n\nSuppose we want to find the total cost after $4.5$ hours.\n\nSince $h=4.5$ is not one of the labeled points on the graph, we cannot read the value directly.\nInstead, we will find the equation that relates $c$ and $h$.\n\nFrom the graph, we can pick out the following coordinates:\n$(0,6),\\: (4,10),\\: (6,12)$\n\nNow, let's put these coordinates into a table:\n\n| $h$ | $c$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $6$ |\n| $4$ | $10$ |\n| $6$ | $12$ |\n\nNotice that each $c$-value is $6$ more than the corresponding $h$-value.\n\n| $h$ | $c$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0 + {\\color{blue}6} = 6$ |\n| $4$ | $4 + {\\color{blue}6} = 10$ |\n| $6$ | $6 + {\\color{blue}6} = 12$ |\n\nHence, the equation that relates $c$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nc = h + {\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nSo, for $h=4.5$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc &= 4.5 + 6 = 10.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $4.5$ hours, the total cost is $\\[math]10.50$." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074948 "When dealing with sums of square roots, we can combine any terms with the same number under the square root symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom 0}.$\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt 5 + 4\\sqrt 5\n$$\n\nRemember that $2\\sqrt 5$ and $4\\sqrt 5$ mean $2\\cdot\\sqrt 5$ and $4\\cdot\\sqrt 5,$ respectively. \n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}+4\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ (2+4)\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{7}-1\\cdot \\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ (2-1)\\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ 1\\cdot \\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{7}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHowever, we *cannot* combine terms when the numbers under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt 3 + \\sqrt 5\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt 3$ and $\\sqrt 5.$" #edb/ref 13194139535245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074949 "The method of subtracting fractions and whole numbers is very similar to the one we use for addition.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the value of\n\n$$\n2-\\dfrac{5}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nWe first write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}6$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{6}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2 \\times {\\color{blue}{6}}}{1\\times{\\color{blue}{6}}} = \\dfrac{12}{6}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{12}{6}}_{2} - \\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same.\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{6} - \\frac{5}{6} = \\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 7 {6}\n$$\n\nWe can always check our answer using a fraction model:\n\n![](images/e6/e67c582daf0ab40f08c2445b1f0527e4316d61114d12ce1aa56ede6c26f3b3a1.png)" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074950 "We use the **standard algorithm** to divide a two-digit number by a one-digit number. To illustrate, let's divide $92$ by $4$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation, as follows:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens. How many times does $4$ go into ${\\color{red}9}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}2}$ times, and\n\n$$\n4\\times {\\color{blue}2} =8\n$$\n\nso there is a remainder of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}9} - 8 = 1\n$$\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}2}$ above the tens, the $8$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the remainder of $1$. Then we bring the ones digit ($2$) down next to the remainder.\n\n| $\\color{blue}2$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}9} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}2$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}9} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}2}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}9} - 8 = 1$Bring down: $2$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones. How many times does $4$ go into ${\\color{red}12}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}3}$ times, and\n\n$$\n4\\times {\\color{blue}3} =12\n$$\n\nso there is no remainder (\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}12} - 12 = 0\n$$\n\n).\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}3}$ above the ones, the $12$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the final remainder of $0$.\n\n| $2$$\\color{blue}3$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}12} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}3}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 12$Subtract: ${\\color{red}12} - 12 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $92$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n92 \\div 4 = 23\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074951 "If there is a remainder after using the standard algorithm, we write the remainder in the answer. To illustrate, let's find the remainder of $95\\div 8\\,$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $8$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$8$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}9} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}5$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}9} \\div 8 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $8 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}9} - 8 = 1$Bring down: $5$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}1$$8$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\fbox{7}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}15} \\div 8 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,7$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $8 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}15} - 8 = \\fbox{7}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $95$, so the division is done. However, we still have a **remainder** of $\\color{blue}7$, so we include this in our final answer.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n95 \\div 8 = {\\color{red}11}\\,\\text{R}\\,{\\color{blue}7}\n$$\n\nHere, $\\color{red}11$ is called the **quotient**." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074952 "We can use the standard algorithm to divide a three-digit number by a one-digit number. To illustrate, let's divide $468$ by $2$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $2$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the hundreds. How many times does $2$ go into ${\\color{red}4}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}2}$ times, and\n\n$$\n2\\times {\\color{blue}2} =4\n$$\n\nso there is a remainder of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}4} - 4 = 0\n$$\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}2}$ above the hundreds, the $4$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the remainder of $0$. Then we bring the tens digit $(6)$ down next to the remainder.\n\n| $\\color{blue}2$$2$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}4} \\div 2 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $2 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 4$Subtract: ${\\color{red}4} - 4 = 0$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens. How many times does $2$ go into ${\\color{red}6}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}3}$ times, and\n\n$$\n2\\times {\\color{blue}3} =6\n$$\n\nso there is a remainder of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}6} - 6 = 0\n$$\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}3}$ above the tens, the $6$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the remainder of $0$. Then, we bring the ones digit $(8)$ down next to the remainder.\n\n| $2$$\\color{blue}3$$2$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{red}6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}6} \\div 2 = {\\color{blue}3}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $2 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}6} - 6 = 0$Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones. How many times does $2$ go into ${\\color{red}8}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}4}$ times, and\n\n$$\n2\\times {\\color{blue}4} =8\n$$\n\nso there is a remainder of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}8} - 8 = 0\n$$\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}4}$ above the ones, the $8$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the final remainder of $0$.\n\n| $2$$3$$\\color{blue}4$$2$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\div 2 = {\\color{blue}4}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}4$Multiply: $2 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 8 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $468$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n468 \\div 2 = 234\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074953 "We can also use the standard algorithm to find the value of $305 \\div 5$. However, because the hundreds digit ($3$) is less than the divisor ($5$), the process will be slightly different.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $5$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\n**Warning**: Notice that $\\color{red}3$ (the number of hundreds in ${\\color{red}3}05$) is less than the divisor $5$, so it doesn't divide. To counter this, we should consider both the hundreds *and* tens. That is, we should divide $\\color{red}30$ by $5$, as follows:\n\n| $\\color{blue}6$$5$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}3} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$0$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}5$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}30} \\div 5 = {\\color{blue}6}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}6$Multiply: $5 \\times {\\color{blue}6} = 30$Subtract: ${\\color{red}30} - 30 = 0$Bring down: $5$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $6$$\\color{blue}1$$5$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$0$$0$$\\color{red}5$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}5} \\div 5 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $5 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 5$Subtract: ${\\color{red}5} - 5 = 0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $305$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n305 \\div 5 = 61\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074954 "We can also use the standard algorithm to find the value of $408 \\div 4$. We will see that the situation here is slightly different. Let's work through it.\n\nAs usual, we start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the hundreds:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}0$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}4} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 4$Subtract: ${\\color{red}4} - 4 = 0$Bring down: $0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}0$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$\\color{red}0$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}0} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}0}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWait! Notice that $\\color{red}0$ is less than the divisor $4$, but not all numbers of the dividend have yet been used! In this case, we need to bring down one more digit:\n\n| $1$$0$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$0$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$0$$\\color{blue}2$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$0$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 8 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $408$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n408 \\div 4 = 102\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074955 "We can also use the standard algorithm to divide a four-digit number by a one-digit number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use the algorithm to divide $4,236$ by $3$. We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $3$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the thousands:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$3$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}2$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}4} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over thousands: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 3$Subtract: ${\\color{red}4} - 3 = 1$Bring down: $2$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the hundreds:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}4$$3$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}3$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}12} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}4}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}4$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 12$Subtract: ${\\color{red}12} - 12 = 0$Bring down: $3$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $1$$4$$\\color{blue}1$$3$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{red}3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}3} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 3$Subtract: ${\\color{red}3} - 3 = 0$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$4$$1$$\\color{blue}2$$3$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$1$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$0$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!$$3$$0$$\\color{red}6$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}6} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}6} - 6 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $4,236$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4,236 \\div 3 = 1,412\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074956 "We can use box models to divide numbers by two-digit numbers.\n\nLet's see how to use a box model by finding the value of $300 \\div 25$.\n\nWe start by writing our division in the following way. We place the dividend in a box and the divisor to the left of the box.\n\n|  |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $25$ | $3$$0$$0$ |  |  |\n\nWe want to find how many times $25$ goes into $300$. But instead of trying to figure this out in a single step, let's break this problem up into a series of simpler steps.\n\nIn our first step, we will pick a multiple of $25$ that is easy to compute and is fairly large but no larger than $300$. We will choose the multiple $25 \\times {\\color{blue}10}$ because we can compute\n\n$$\n25 \\times {\\color{blue}10} = 250\n$$\n\nquickly and the result is no larger than $300$.\n\nTo organize our work so far, we write $\\color{blue}10$ above the box and subtract $25 \\times {\\color{blue}10}$ from $300$ inside the box.\n\n|  | $\\color{blue}10$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $25$ | $3$$0$$0$$-\\!$$2$$5$$0$$\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$ |  |  |\n\nThe result of the subtraction is ${\\color{red}50}$. This indicates that if we take $25$ a total of ${\\color{blue}10}$ times, then it will fit into $300$ with $\\color{red}50$ left over.\n\nNow, we repeat the process with a dividend of $\\color{red}50$ instead of $300$. To start the next round, we write $\\color{red}50$ in a new box to the right.\n\n|  | $10$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $25$ | $3$$0$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$5$$0$$\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$ | $\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$ |  |\n\nAgain, we are looking for a multiple of $25$ that is easy to compute and is no larger than ${\\color{red}50}$. We will choose the multiple $25 \\times {\\color{blue}2}$ because we can compute\n\n$$\n25 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 50\n$$\n\nquickly and the result is no larger than ${\\color{red}50}$.\n\nAs before, we write $\\color{blue}2$ above the box and subtract $25 \\times {\\color{blue}2}$ from ${\\color{red}50}$ inside the box.\n\n|  | $10$ | $\\color{blue}2$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $25$ | $3$$0$$0$$-\\!$$2$$5$$0$$\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$ | $\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |  |\n\nWe get $\\fbox{0}$, so the division is done.\n\nTo find the quotient $300 \\div 25$, all we need to do is add the numbers on top of the boxes:\n\n$$\n10+2=12\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n300 \\div 25 = 12\\,\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074957 "In this lesson, we'll consider real-life situations where we have to multiply fractions by whole numbers.\n\nSuppose that Justin, a chef, has $8$ large tins of tomatoes. If each tin of tomatoes weighs $\\dfrac{5}{4}\\,\\textrm{lb},$ how many pounds of tomatoes does Justin have in total?\n\nTo find out the total weight of tomatoes, we need to multiply $\\dfrac{5}{4}\\,\\textrm{lb}$ by $8.$  \n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n8 \\times \\dfrac54= \\dfrac{8\\times 5}{4} = \\dfrac{40}4\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by interpreting the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{4} =  40 \\div 4 = 10\n$$\n\nSo, in total, Justin has $10\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of tomatoes." #edb/ref 13194139535005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074958 "Fraction multiplication occurs regularly in real-life situations.\n\nFor example, suppose that a bag contains ![](images/bc/bcc2ab102a29294d71449d99af1cf1a88aae88047223f829175086f7a6cd5e10.svg) of flour. If Nancy used ![](images/c0/c0db422914dd04be9adf05af649ec06419e80d8425cd0a85f6105cef7cc447b5.svg) of the bag to make a pie, how much flour did she use to make the pie?\n\nTo find out how much flour Nancy used, we need to multiply ![](images/16/1627bb3010ada55ed941966d38081f1a6c1394c07ca78b94585f8c97e4bbc138.svg) by ![](images/d9/d989525f1462386565e344f7e9a41d48cea1e85148f9f5aba25268f589a8bcbc.svg)\n\nTo multiply these two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n![](images/58/58824e95f00448b5c7d509f0781a367506ccf37fa018f2a0ba373c74191f1bb6.svg)\n\nTherefore, Nancy used ![](images/fd/fd1a8efcb6ae625f2259a43e8ffeb93d486d409af6175b6e52380f4ccd488af7.svg) kilograms of flour." #edb/ref 13194139535160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074959 "Fraction multiplication can often be interpreted as the area of a part of a unit square.\n\nLet's consider the unit square below. It has sides of length $1$ unit, divided into lots of little tiles.\n\n![](images/4d/4d6dd44238dba1130ff2c8dcf9f98097ddd150f8aedb1af6c7a7ab0f2a7f949e.png)\n\nEach side of the unit square is divided into $\\color{blue}4$ equal pieces. So, each side of a tile is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nunits long.\n\n![](images/be/be6e67444be087a83e7c9d3c718210c03f17065f618f736a4a8f8ec7f2bb2593.png)\n\nThe area of each tile is the product of the two side lengths. Therefore, the area of each tile is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\times \\dfrac{1}{4}\\;\\textrm{square units}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074960 "Another way of computing the area of a part of a unit square is to realize that we can find the area of a single tile using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{area of a single tile} = \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{number of tiles}}\n$$\n\nKnowing this, what is the area of the shaded region below, in square units?\n\n![](images/64/64679059ef622fe8cc35e16725f93eeac561bb82864582f5b8e21614c43558a5.png)\n\nFirst, note that the region is $7$ tiles long and $7$ tiles high.\n\n![](images/70/709e8343f42bf0b38ec8b0384ffe5a133d8ee1ffb590031d4b3bcd5e90bdcfac.png)\n\nSo there are a total of\n\n$$\n7 \\times 7 = 49\n$$\n\nsmall square tiles. Therefore, the area of each tile is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{49}\n$$\n\nsquare units.\n\nSince $10$ tiles are shaded, the total shaded area is:\n\n$$\n10\\times\\dfrac{1}{49} = \\dfrac{10}{49} \\,\\textrm{square units}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074961 "Instead of counting the number of tiles in a shaded region, we can find the length of its sides and multiply them together.\nSo, what is the area of the shaded part below?\n\n![](images/ee/ee0ce39f12b80fe3567a95b4319883f8499c69b3a0238e85b996524a33f91fed.png)\n\nFirst, notice that each side of the unit square is divided into $\\color{blue}7$ equal pieces. So, each of the smaller tiles has sides that are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}7}\n$$\n\nunits long.\n\nThe shaded region is $4$ tiles long and $6$ tiles high, which means that it has dimensions\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{7}\n$$\n\nunits by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{7}\n$$\n\nunits.\n\n![](images/93/93df4b2349db7e6fc2f45e82df1cc36270404adc0897d887f5a243ce9fa3b71d.png)\n\nFinally, we find the area of the shaded rectangle by multiplying the lengths of the sides. So, the area is:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{7} \\times \\dfrac{6}{7}\\,\\,\\textrm{square units}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074962 "There are many ways to divide decimals. One such way is to write the division as a fraction and then multiply the numerator and denominator by a power of $10$ so that both become whole numbers. Then, we carry out the division as usual.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use this method to find the value of $4.5\\div 0.5$.\n\n**Step 1.** First, we write the division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4.5}{0.5}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Then, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4.5\\times 10}{0.5\\times 10} = \\dfrac{45}{5}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Finally, we solve the resulting division problem. In this case, we have\n\n$$\n45 \\div 5 = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4.5 \\div 0.5 = 9 \\,\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074963 "When using long division to divide whole numbers, it's often appropriate to round the final answer.\n\nFor instance, let's find the value of $58 \\div 7$, rounded to the nearest tenth. We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\,. \\,} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{black}8} \\: \\ldots$$7$$\\phantom{\\mid}\\!\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$6$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$4$$\\vdots$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe could divide further, but since we want to round to $1$ decimal place (the nearest tenth), we stop after finding the quotient to $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n58 \\div 7 = 8.28 \\ldots\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest tenth. The hundredths place is $8$, which is greater than $5$, so we round up:\n$8.28 \\ldots \\rightarrow 8.3$" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074964 "We can use the standard algorithm to divide decimals by whole numbers.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute $7.2 \\div 3$.\n\nFirst, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\,\\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.} 2{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, we carry out the long division process as usual:\n\n| $\\,2 {\\,\\,. \\,} 4$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\,\\,. \\,} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7.2 \\div 3 = 2.4 \\,\n$$\n\n**Note:** The \"standard algorithm\" is the same as \"long division.\" The two phrases can be used interchangeably." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074965 "We can also use the standard algorithm to divide decimals by other decimals. The only difference is that we first need to find an equivalent division problem where the divisor is a whole number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute $1.11\\div 0.3 \\,$. First, we write this division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1.11}{0.3}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$ so that we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1.11 \\times 10}{0.3\\times 10} = \\dfrac{11.1}{3}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the division problem is equivalent to $11.1 \\div 3$. We can compute this using long division as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,\\,. \\,} 7$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\,\\,. \\,} 1}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$2$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$1$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we find that\n\n$$\n11.1 \\div 3 = 3.7\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n1.11 \\div 0.3 = 3.7 \\,\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074966 "We can write ratios as fractions by placing the first number over the second number.\n\nSo, what ratio describes the number of rectangles to triangles below?\n\n![](images/25/25426db008d4b246f36959f51433e0a5cebe609fc01f9539d4f45af353919261.png)\n\nFirst, we count the number of rectangles. We see that there are $\\color{blue}4$ rectangles.\n\nThen, we count the number of triangles. We see that there are $\\color{red}3$ triangles.\n\nSo the ratio of rectangles to triangles is ${\\color{blue}4}:{\\color{red}3}.$ As a fraction, this is $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}3}.$\n\nWe interpret this number to mean that there are $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}3}$ rectangles for each triangle." #edb/ref 13194139534736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074967 "Sometimes, we are given a ratio and want to find an amount. In these cases, it's helpful to use equivalent fractions. \n\nFor example, if the ratio of cows to sheep on Margaret's farm is $11:7$, and she has $22$ cows, how many sheep are there on the farm?\n\nTo solve this, we start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}11}:{\\color{red}7}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}11}{\\color{red}7}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of cows and the denominator represents the number of sheep.\n\nSince there are $22$ cows on the farm, we would like a numerator of $22.$ We make this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}11}\\times 2}{{\\color{red}7}\\times 2} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}22}}{{\\color{red}14}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is now the number of sheep on the farm if there are $22$ cows. Therefore, on Margaret's farm, there are $\\color{red}14$ sheep." #edb/ref 13194139535279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074968 "Remember that we can place equivalent ratios in a ratio table, like the one below.\n\n| $\\color{blue}x$ | $\\color{red}y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\color{blue}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ | $\\color{red}4$ |\n| $\\color{blue}4$ | $\\color{red}8$ |\n\nBy setting the values in the left column as $\\color{blue}x$-coordinates and the values in the right column as the corresponding $\\color{red}y$-coordinates, we can graph the points to represent a relationship between the ratios.\n\nIn this case, we have the following ordered pairs: $({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}2}),$ $({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}4}),$ $({\\color{blue}4},{\\color{red}8}).$\n\nPlotting these points on a coordinate plane, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/0c/0c4abb8d462730ed9b532feeca1e0a800f5376a978bfc799afb9d9a927ff310d.png)\n\nConnecting the points gives us a straight line! Plotting equivalent ratios will *always* give a straight line. Furthermore, the straight line will *always* pass through the origin $(0,0).$" #edb/ref 13194139535005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074969 "We can illustrate ratios with a **tape diagram**, where each box represents a unit.\n\nThe tape diagram below shows the ratio between the number of black hair ties and white hair ties there are in the package Anabelle bought.\n\n![](images/15/15c9b0b47bd0e6fdca99ab67df4bdc7e9202978128770df717bb347e463cfb33.png)\n\nIn this diagram, each box represents a hair tie.\nSo, what is the ratio of white hair ties to black hair ties?\n\nThe tape diagram for white hair ties shows $\\color{blue}5$ boxes, so Anabelle has $\\color{blue}5$ white hair ties.\n\nLikewise, the tape diagram for black hair ties contains $\\color{red}6$ boxes, so she has $\\color{red}6$ black hair ties.\n\nTherefore, the ratio of white hair ties to black hair ties is ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}6}.$" #edb/ref 13194139535344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074970 "A **unit rate** is a comparison of two quantities where one of the terms has a quantity of $1.$ This means the ratio, in fraction form, has a denominator of $1.$\n\nFor instance, suppose Michael earned $\\[math] 200$ by selling $4$ identical tires.\n\nThe ratio of dollars to tires is ${\\color{blue}200}:{\\color{red}4}.$ Writing the ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200}}{{\\color{red}4}} \\, .\n$$\n\nThe unit rate is how many dollars Michael got for $1$ tire.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200} \\div 4}{{\\color{red}4} \\div 4} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}50}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nThis is the unit rate. In this case, we say that Michael got $\\color{blue}50$ dollars *per* tire." #edb/ref 13194139535279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074971 "In some tape diagrams, each box can represent more than one unit.\n\nFor instance, the ratio of pins to paper clips in a case is given by the tape diagram below. If there are $15$ pins in the case, how many paper clips are there in total?\n\n![](images/82/82f94d7377ea3923ffdd825de324b128cd8b4f102070bf6890dee2cbc619657e.png)\n\nWe are told that there are $15$ pins in the case, but the tape diagram for pins shows $3$ boxes. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents $15 \\div 3 = 5$ units. \n\nNow, we fill the number $5$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](images/7d/7d8d1e22ee067588593b7b08442dd2a44142f9f684d84809056ee0ffb4b2dfc0.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ boxes corresponding to paper clips, and each box represents $5$ units, the total number of paper clips is\n\n$$\n4 \\times 5 = 20 \\, .\n$$\n\nSo, there are $20$ paper clips in Mrs. Robinson's case." #edb/ref 13194139535344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074972 "A **percentage** is another way of expressing a fraction that's used often in everyday situations. The word **percent** means \"out of $100$.\"\n\nLet's take a look at the square below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c2fd8e09cd4c0e7d6160c96c29b644c22e17c09d80e025e3cced2b391a20c94.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- the square has been divided into $100$ equal parts, and\n- $\\color{blue}50$ of those parts are shaded.\n\nSince $\\color{blue}50$ out of $100$ parts are shaded, we say that \"$\\color{blue}50$ percent of the square is shaded.\"\n\nWe have a special symbol that we use instead of the word percent. This symbol is written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Large{\\%}\n$$\n\nUsing this symbol, we say that \"${\\color{blue}{5}0}\\%$ of the square is shaded.\"" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074973 "When more than $100$ pieces are shaded in a model where $100$ pieces represent a whole, the percentage will be greater than $100\\%$.\n\nLet's take a look at the model below, where one large square represents one whole:\n\n![](images/5f/5fcb68226ada21bec7e2f4dd90fd5c9fde2c43578e1611e0faf85ddb580b5f34.png)\n\nIn this model, $170$ tiles are shaded. Therefore, this model represents $170\\%$." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074974 "How do we find the percentage represented by a fraction model when the model isn't split into $100$ equal pieces?\n\nThe answer is to express the fraction as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $100$.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the model below:\n\n![](images/17/17f55289981c45c7473462f06592a9577d692a9efba462b1101022ae8a630a4f.png)\n\nThis model represents the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nTo make an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $100$, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $20$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 20}{5 \\times 20} = \\dfrac{80}{100}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{80}{100}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $80\\%$, we conclude that our fraction model represents $80\\%$.\n\nWe can also use equivalent fractions to construct a simplified fraction model for a given percent. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074975 "We can represent two-variable equations using graphs. For example, consider the following equation:\n\n$$\ny = x + 5\n$$\n\nA point $(x, y)$ is a solution to this equation if substituting the $x$-value into the equation gives the correct $y$-value.\n\nTo find some solution points, we can choose several $x$-values and compute the corresponding $y$-values.\nNotice that:\n\n- If $x=0$, then $y=0 + 5 = 5$.\n- If $x=1$, then $y=1 + 5 = 6$.\n- If $x=2$, then $y=2 + 5 = 7$.\n- If $x=3$, then $y=3 + 5 = 8$.\n\nWe can organize these results in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ |\n| $3$ | $8$ |\n\nSo, we have the following points:\n$(0,5),\\: (1,6),\\: (2,7),\\: (3,8)$\n\nTo graph the equation, we plot these points on the coordinate plane and draw a straight line through them.\n\n![](images/c6/c67b80c4aa1ed18929337792c7d9cdeea89f8b937f2e1fa29be767fcf627eaff.png)\n\nEvery point $(x,y)$ on the line satisfies the equation." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074976 "Two intersecting lines are **perpendicular** if they form a right angle (an angle that measures $90^\\circ$). \n\n![](images/ee/ee6258a8a148ae3a8f4ce63321bf9f4dfa955eb0a90b332477e6cc933cad7b74.png)\n\nFor example, the two lines above are perpendicular because the angle $\\angle BOD$ is a right angle.\n\nThe opposite is also true: if two lines are perpendicular, then they meet at a right angle.\n\nAs you may have noticed already, when two lines are perpendicular, they don't form only one right angle. All the four angles formed by the intersection are right angles!\n\n![](images/15/152aa56c4fd669e417cb20f1bef8fcec7e7ea34e0be9e44e7acad3ac490e5713.png)" #edb/ref 13194139535300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074977 "Let's look at the following right triangle.\n\n![](images/46/466ec5d41b5e06a54cef9e1d840ad6ef0b22a479b81fa56b9612eb8cc03deae2.png)\n\nHow can we find its area?\n\nIt turns out that:\n\n> The area of a right triangle is half the area of the corresponding rectangle:\n> $\\text{Area of Triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\text{(Area of Rectangle)}$.\n\nIn our case, the corresponding rectangle has dimensions $8\\:\\text{m}$ by $6\\:\\text{m}$.\n\n![](images/4d/4d2dbaba3d6a4064901bb4dd9bd444c32ef1163f21d9e6cbec2ac5cf5962c39b.png)\n\nThe area of the rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea of Rectangle &= 8m \\cdot 6m \\\\\n&= (8 \\cdot 6)(m \\cdot m) \\\\\n&= 48m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area of Triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 48 = 24 \\: \\text{m}^2\n$$\n\nLet’s see more examples." #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074978 "The distance between two points in the coordinate plane is the number of units between them. Let's explore two methods for calculating the horizontal distance between two points with the points $(1,1)$ and $(5,1).$\n\n**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFirst, we plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/19/196c50295e8d8a95084c31226fb9319ede7b70603d894b6939e4615df583c29c.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $4$ units, so the distance equals $4.$\n\n![](images/4b/4b531e29616f3e8d7ea0c65761e59cfea224b9c0e756c68b7647c7271dc09493.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/6d/6d5c6760e15098dc381e639cd806b6410f2fa6d9049d11d5467427635c9872b6.png)\n\nThe point $({\\color{blue}{1}},1)$ is at a distance of $\\color{blue}{1}$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $({\\color{red}{5}},1)$ is at a distance of $\\color{red}{5}$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is ${\\color{red}{5}} - {\\color{blue}{1}} = 4.$" #edb/ref 13194139534868 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
